function is concave up over the given interval.    \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave up on $[0,3]$ is the one shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/bf44f59745d8fefd48ed9106bbfcbc7e59eba1df0cf0e49fdcb6ca3e9c5a1db6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bec73486-156f-5124-97b4-58f65ea8430d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0714a1d-dab9-5277-9eda-ced86236f64e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334153} {:answer/id #uuid "57866f5c-40e1-5450-a965-5bcf11b3c496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334154} {:answer/id #uuid "adb03fc7-8074-53dd-8384-7727d54abff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334155} {:answer/id #uuid "bcdf8516-8c3c-5b22-a90a-8a436cc55863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334156} {:answer/id #uuid "c806ecea-9b5b-51a2-8ff0-dcdee46b4412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c806ecea-9b5b-51a2-8ff0-dcdee46b4412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334157} :answer-field/id #uuid "47ec45e6-b931-5f94-b529-28778acf145f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c429ee66-b024-55a3-ae66-fa1cf23eaccf" :question/math-academy-id "q-116296" :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[1,5]=[1,2]\\cup [2,5],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3$ | $7$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/8fe84e0056eaa8320b4824740a259226b1a18ff1b64ba648d8740d6cac8703fc.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)+f(5)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+3}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{3+7}{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2+15 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bec73486-156f-5124-97b4-58f65ea8430d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dfb11b4-1238-5036-a856-f7491620e5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334160} {:answer/id #uuid "29885906-9716-5bac-b33b-607ecaaee031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334161} {:answer/id #uuid "389e8326-7153-5ab7-9a60-208f62238518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334162} {:answer/id #uuid "f94f567f-26ba-5840-a883-d70712b5221b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334163} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d5fc3b-8344-5ae7-ade1-e810c9ecc9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29885906-9716-5bac-b33b-607ecaaee031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334161} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d18456-cfd7-5bab-b94f-74cb9ea5d189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5abc5349-5c55-5963-9a71-982535e3090d" :question/math-academy-id "q-116300" :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-3,2]=[-3,-1]\\cup [-1,0]\\cup [0,2],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $6$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/25dc419eb5711b0d990909c611a6eacb2754285fb45dc0d969207e63dc09ba12.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(-1)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-1)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{2+4}{2}\\cdot (-1-(-3))+\\frac{4+8}{2}\\cdot (0-(-1))+\\frac{8+6}{2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+6+14 \\\\  & =26\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "903cd6d7-18f2-5b53-a8e9-9aaef5589549" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60bd81ef-dc3b-53ff-8610-de655dd68353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$26$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334167} {:answer/id #uuid "c6631b3c-46da-58cf-9101-9c8d243d67f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334168} {:answer/id #uuid "32a002ac-3e81-5923-8ccc-f92ad0c26792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$22$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334169} {:answer/id #uuid "84844895-4bf8-5e89-97c7-8acb5f54e244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$32$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334170} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4d52c3-11a4-5fc7-91d6-c823b2ffec79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$11$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32a002ac-3e81-5923-8ccc-f92ad0c26792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$22$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334169} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd18512-aba0-5661-a3a8-bf18b3dcb0cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff130200-2cc7-564b-a515-b5f1a6777878" :question/math-academy-id "q-116618" :question/problem "Lisa is selling orange juice. The following table shows the rate, in liters per hour, at which it is sold.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hour})$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $c(t)\\,(\\text{lts/hour})$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[1,2]\\cup [2,5],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total number of liters of juice sold by Lisa over a 4 hours period." :question/worked-solution "In this context, we have $2$ trapezoids, and the area $A$ under the curve $y=c(t)$ represents the total number of liters sold over a $4$ period.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{c(1)+c(2)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{1}+\\frac{c(2)+c(5)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{3+5}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{5+7}{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =22\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Lisa sold approximately $22\\,\\text{liters}$ over a $4$ hours period."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32793ffd-b4c6-58af-9f6f-69c756ed7f1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule with Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c18582-9114-5864-8e63-ed0a66feeb4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08c18582-9114-5864-8e63-ed0a66feeb4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334174} :answer-field/id #uuid "12deef7f-43f7-50cf-b65e-fdd89b0b57ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29c3beea-8795-545d-afd1-d68722bc0433" :question/math-academy-id "q-347050" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/eda6639ced5b182d7a05017883cf15dfbc1ce4b11e4ad3161d0ed6f9d2ab982c.png)\n\nEstimate the area $A$ under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+9x$ over the interval $[0,9]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+9x$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,9],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=9.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{9-0}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(0)+2(f(3)+f(6))+f(9)]\\cdot 3.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $18$ | $18$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [0+2(18+18)+0]\\cdot 3=108\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[0,9],$ the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore the approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bec73486-156f-5124-97b4-58f65ea8430d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b862279-c81e-5ce0-9755-09571f2eb2b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334177} {:answer/id #uuid "be9738eb-cd9f-5d58-8e26-0b783ca12442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be9738eb-cd9f-5d58-8e26-0b783ca12442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334178} :answer-field/id #uuid "41bb622c-d994-5c6e-ac14-36b7470868d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8541eab3-b263-590c-8b67-17f74278ebca" :question/math-academy-id "q-347099" :question/problem "| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $5$ | $6$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,15]=[0,4]\\cup [4,7]\\cup [7,15],\n$$\n\napproximate the area $A$ under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/a42b281338ce0fc7d64677b260f6c0d8cdaa47855afe35bae0c21fde750a39d5.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(4)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(4)+f(7)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(7)+f(15)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+5}{2}\\cdot (4-0)+\\frac{5+6}{2}\\cdot (7-4)+\\frac{6+2}{2}\\cdot (15-7) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4+\\frac{11}{2}\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =12+\\frac{33}{2}+32 \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "903cd6d7-18f2-5b53-a8e9-9aaef5589549" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230a3554-140a-5223-9cf7-ae443b7c570e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "297" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "230a3554-140a-5223-9cf7-ae443b7c570e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "297" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334181} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a04e898-02d7-5ce1-9c8e-cd1d485c4b83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "017ebb65-acc5-5cb0-bdcd-469277cb48d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-347170" :question/problem "A solar array’s power output rate (in kilowatts) is modeled by $p(t)=-2{t}^{2}+20t.$ Approximate the total number of kilowatt-hours of energy produced in the first nine hours using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\nThe solar array produced  \n{{field-1}}\nkilowatt-hours of energy." :question/worked-solution "In this context, the area $A$ under the curve $y=p(t)$ over the interval $[0,9]$ represents the total number of kilowatt-hours of energy produced in the first nine hours.\n\nThe general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[p(a)+2(p({t}_{1})+p({t}_{2})+…+p({t}_{n-1}))+p(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,9],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=9.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{9-0}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[p(0)+2(p(3)+p(6))+p(9)]\\cdot 3.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $t$ and $p(t):$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(t)$ | $0$ | $42$ | $48$ | $18$ |\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[0+2(42+48)+18]\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[198]\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =297.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solar array produced $297$ kilowatt-hours of energy in the first nine hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32793ffd-b4c6-58af-9f6f-69c756ed7f1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule with Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ab4f30d-e1a6-548a-84c7-1f52e6196661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334184} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb8588f-6c58-5d5b-a146-abce99257a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fb8588f-6c58-5d5b-a146-abce99257a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334185} :answer-field/id #uuid "e36d7e31-9c5f-514f-90e0-a077cfb1702a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0501acb-52f3-5fb0-8ac6-9605e9e2a3bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-364578" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/732c560cfab8b78b2f37ae6623567be2ec2934b34d9b5b40d1c9bebcf0f9e92c.png)\n\nEstimate the area $A$ under the curve $y={x}^{2}+2$ over the interval $[0,6]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,6],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=6.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{6-0}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(0)+2(f(2)+f(4))+f(6)]\\cdot 2.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $6$ | $18$ | $38$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [2+2(6+18)+38]\\cdot 2=88\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[0,6],$ the trapezoids lie over the function, and therefore the approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32793ffd-b4c6-58af-9f6f-69c756ed7f1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule with Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d680c8a0-cf89-5be7-b9d0-3c294402217d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d680c8a0-cf89-5be7-b9d0-3c294402217d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334188} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f698a13-3cdc-536b-b743-ff3e82edba28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbf3c1fd-e8de-5eee-b6f0-4c9d5d50fb48" :question/math-academy-id "q-364580" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/24fd0ccf835405c3da099e0993a3cebe6711ecd2f0c4c1e960275be3dad032aa.png)\n\nEstimate the area under the curve $y=\\frac{120}{{x}^{2}-1}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $2$ trapezoids of equal height. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{120}{{x}^{2}-1}$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[2,4],$ so we have $a=2$ and $b=4.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{4-2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(2)+2f(3)+f(4)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $40$ | $15$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [40+2(15)+8]\\cdot 1=39\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[2,4],$ the trapezoids lie over the function, and therefore approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2960d2d6-7354-5f2a-80cc-8d514465455f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether the Trapezoidal Rule Sum Gives an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dadc1170-cd8e-502d-ab7c-938273fee4f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/b22233c43c15eecb9990275dfb52103bcfe6e4136bad3964df32365fc11e175a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334191} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe65192-efaf-5d9c-983b-25e1d0d30c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/d31008777c9ef9f1f72175f922a2d7c42660796e8c3ffa1dc29198727e8c5734.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334192} {:answer/id #uuid "caa4c41b-46fe-5ccf-b12e-dcbd534e8c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/dffcd78690e3f4c9dc17eb41c4a54d71f65ca911f781fa8419a6c6b1cd1ead88.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334193} {:answer/id #uuid "4b023ebe-5645-599f-9f45-0cb3d01e1d81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/9b5c4d4ad6c2dfa8fd3262360e567a688aa371c95f4b340678b08e4206c5e114.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334194} {:answer/id #uuid "34a24d4f-b52f-50b9-aa69-2dba0b053131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/fe9e4782c6fa8a6f7ae21b79bdab75434aaf8c47c74675d44043e543b9fd7d04.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe65192-efaf-5d9c-983b-25e1d0d30c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/d31008777c9ef9f1f72175f922a2d7c42660796e8c3ffa1dc29198727e8c5734.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334192} :answer-field/id #uuid "7556ddf2-0d11-5552-9691-9df87260b6e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70654faf-6d85-5072-a4d5-744e528c3f2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49158" :question/problem "For which of the following graphs would the trapezoidal rule give an overestimate of the area under the curve between $x=1$ and $x=4?$" :question/worked-solution "The trapezoidal rule overestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave up over the given interval.    \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave up on $[1,4]$ is the one shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/d31008777c9ef9f1f72175f922a2d7c42660796e8c3ffa1dc29198727e8c5734.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2960d2d6-7354-5f2a-80cc-8d514465455f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether the Trapezoidal Rule Sum Gives an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a91c6efd-cb3d-573a-a4a0-42cc1bbd87f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/e048c1c1052238e83f1c74864e4806371e0fa8c2c2f3499b6e244614f14aeb76.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334198} {:answer/id #uuid "d822b911-42e8-59ef-afe8-56b73998b836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/546afec9a47772181b933cb26afda052e5f039226c02db0c565b3e06b0418458.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334199} {:answer/id #uuid "0e48d288-a9f6-56d4-96bc-51fe8ff2e396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/025a9032c02e58fba378b41936f3f7191251bd14ba1e63c2d199a5352ce7494b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334200} {:answer/id #uuid "5983fc8b-57bb-531d-bc87-c33bb1bb5cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/d11a3557cddc21f5f20f4c85374fa2ca7bddf70307671542f228874af64585d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334201} {:answer/id #uuid "a1033193-e2a8-5234-8c41-558d547c0871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/2f966ea4bc8d2257a9082347c1bf774cbb40f0c2b5561a0d051fe088bdab43d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1033193-e2a8-5234-8c41-558d547c0871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/2f966ea4bc8d2257a9082347c1bf774cbb40f0c2b5561a0d051fe088bdab43d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334202} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d95e6ea-cf16-5167-9d48-1dc5150a9b9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f7cda04-70ae-570b-9bd1-8ebb0e1d2784" :question/math-academy-id "q-49165" :question/problem "For which of the following graphs would the trapezoidal rule give an underestimate of the area under the curve between $x=-2$ and $x=2?$" :question/worked-solution "The trapezoidal rule underestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave down over the given interval.     \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave down on $[-2,2]$ is the one shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/2f966ea4bc8d2257a9082347c1bf774cbb40f0c2b5561a0d051fe088bdab43d2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "903cd6d7-18f2-5b53-a8e9-9aaef5589549" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c60bea12-49c3-527f-81d8-6db076320f40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334205} {:answer/id #uuid "1c5425f2-376b-56e1-995a-7c03bdb8e43d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334206} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb94d95-ba36-571a-96c2-77c21d2006a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{24.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334207} {:answer/id #uuid "e6358295-4e32-51f4-a961-883434cecb15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334208} {:answer/id #uuid "cae62c33-68ab-5837-8372-1ce39e78c87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c60bea12-49c3-527f-81d8-6db076320f40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334205} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1854635-102f-5870-9d42-7884e785e958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5be4a2da-fb76-54b9-819f-4f8cf046d700" :question/math-academy-id "q-49238" :question/problem "The rate, in degrees Farhenheit per month, at which the average midday temperature in New York City varies, does so according to the function $R(t)={t}^{3}+3,$ where $t$ is the number of months since the measurements began. Estimate the total increase in air temperature over the first three months using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height." :question/worked-solution "In this context, the area $A$ under the curve $y=R(t)$ over the interval $[0,3]$ represents the total increase in air temperature over the first three months.\n\nThe general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[R(a)+2(R({t}_{1})+R({t}_{2})+⋯+R({t}_{n-1}))+R(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,3],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=3.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{3-0}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[R(0)+2(R(1)+R(2))+R(3)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $t$ and $R(t):$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $11$ | $30$ |\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[3+2(4+11)+30]\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{2} \\\\  & =31.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total air temperature increase is approximately ${31.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "903cd6d7-18f2-5b53-a8e9-9aaef5589549" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a0915ac-d0c5-52e9-be1d-358ab643ad99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334212} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6434dd-80e9-5c65-a561-cf3fa659e3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334213} {:answer/id #uuid "688bbd9f-757a-5f14-881d-524639cf9645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334214} {:answer/id #uuid "57738d1c-a2ee-53d0-9e42-f41f692e8e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334215} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bdc49b-d082-594b-8de1-018b6944b54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a0915ac-d0c5-52e9-be1d-358ab643ad99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334212} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bb03d1f-a98f-5a87-8b3a-32048ad183fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab3c0204-d78f-5315-ac2e-2155f33a9b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-51076" :question/problem "The following table shows the rate $r(t),$ in centimeters per hour, at which rain is falling during a two-hour period of a certain day.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(hours)}$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r\\,\\text{(centimeters/hour)}$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nApproximate the total amount of rainfall during this period using the trapezoidal rule with $4$ trapezoids of equal height." :question/worked-solution "In this context, the area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ over the interval $[0,2]$ represents the total amount of rainfall during the two-hour period.\n\nThe general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[r(a)+2(r({t}_{1})+r({t}_{2})+⋯+r({t}_{n-1}))+r(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=4.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,2],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=2.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{2-0}{4}=0.5.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[r(0)+2(r(0.5)+r(1)+r(1.5))+r(2)]\\cdot 0.5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers from the given table in the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[3+2(4+3+3)+1]\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.25(24) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $6\\,\\text{cm}$ of rain fell during the given two-hour period."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "903cd6d7-18f2-5b53-a8e9-9aaef5589549" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e490f31-0082-5670-a54b-3575e1b7d4f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$425$ miles" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334219} {:answer/id #uuid "f13f3974-b92f-52b1-a187-2ec78a10997c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$370$ miles" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334220} {:answer/id #uuid "7db1798e-9d66-5d90-8428-15207558c52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$390$ miles" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334221} {:answer/id #uuid "5795d60e-135e-53f7-9888-46c421db4e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$480$ miles" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334222} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca341f0-a694-5e2a-832e-c0fe3dea3137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$300$ miles" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e490f31-0082-5670-a54b-3575e1b7d4f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$425$ miles" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334219} :answer-field/id #uuid "3752c1ac-9857-5e9b-ae75-a35234cb2425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f29b6971-06ec-5eb0-87ec-6449e746727e" :question/math-academy-id "q-51077" :question/problem "The following table shows the speed $V(t),$ in miles per hour, at which an airplane flies at certain times since its $2$-hour journey began.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $V\\,(\\text{miles/hour})$ | $150$ | $250$ | $200$ |\n\nApproximate the total distance traveled by the plane using the trapezoidal rule with $2$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "In this context, the area $A$ under the curve $y=V(t)$ over the interval $[0,2]$ represents the total distance traveled by the plane.\n\nThe general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[V(a)+2(V({t}_{1})+V({t}_{2})+⋯+V({t}_{n-1}))+V(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,2],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=2.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{2-0}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[V(0)+2V(1)+V(2)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers from the given table into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[150+2(250)+200]\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{850}{2} \\\\  & =425.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total distance traveled by the plane is approximately $425$ miles."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32793ffd-b4c6-58af-9f6f-69c756ed7f1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule with Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9521d834-3f19-52a8-998d-a0bd8e17bac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334226} {:answer/id #uuid "48fa0f73-9ace-525f-a614-4249c2be74ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334227} {:answer/id #uuid "5dba6fc1-6e24-5a4b-b593-6cf0f2ff7c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334228} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8ce672-c5ca-5fac-9ba7-2a4a297a3282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334229} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2608f2-7537-5eb0-9a77-9f9ea7c7f2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad2608f2-7537-5eb0-9a77-9f9ea7c7f2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334230} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5dbbb38-3a0a-5459-b671-64510feb9786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47f34064-59b1-5054-b709-399abe1fd2c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-85636" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/89426d77a95ffb1a4780484ed1e1ad0531ccd1c4022419ef2c573a08922bf7eb.png)\n\nEstimate the area under the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[-1,2]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\n*Note: figure not drawn to scale.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4-{x}^{2}$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[-1,2],$ so we have $a=-1$ and $b=2.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(-1)+2(f(0)+f(1))+f(2)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[3+2(4+3)+0]\\cdot 1=\\frac{17}{2}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave down* over the interval $[-1,2],$ the trapezoids lie under the function, and therefore approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32793ffd-b4c6-58af-9f6f-69c756ed7f1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule with Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d642411-6d7b-5f4d-aa5f-41bf4fb261ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334233} {:answer/id #uuid "67b7f806-fc4c-5824-b1e9-4ca54b1de507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334234} {:answer/id #uuid "41e0c0b7-602a-57a5-99fe-fb0d33e79c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334235} {:answer/id #uuid "63440777-63fc-5f7c-b823-f1600cf39486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334236} {:answer/id #uuid "378681d7-89c2-5a75-868c-106a07d6699c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67b7f806-fc4c-5824-b1e9-4ca54b1de507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334234} :answer-field/id #uuid "9833acc1-251e-5c62-b915-3cdc414c8d11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d712440d-8425-5c56-8219-fb7fd3e0a895" :question/math-academy-id "q-85637" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/0c5c97425f6a03a55067f8848b7bae1c20d2db3e924bf9aaa277c99c34c65364.png)\n\nEstimate the area under the curve $y=1-{x}^{3}$ over the interval $[-2,0]$ using the trapezoidal rule with $2$ trapezoids of equal height.\n\n*Note: figure not drawn to scale.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=1-{x}^{3}$ and let $A$ denote the required area. The general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(a)+2(f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})+…+f({x}_{n-1}))+f(b)]Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=2.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[-2,0],$ so we have $a=-2$ and $b=0.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{0-(-2)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[f(-2)+2f(-1)+f(0)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $x$ and $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $9$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[9+2(2)+1]\\cdot 1=7\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[-2,0],$ the trapezoids lie over the function, and therefore approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2960d2d6-7354-5f2a-80cc-8d514465455f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether the Trapezoidal Rule Sum Gives an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce8bdec1-5bfc-5ae3-9e65-266c2b677aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/546a9769660a9a036d68e67e0a5e0d2fb8af8a498fb944afcff73c869c750de0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334240} {:answer/id #uuid "85c9aaf7-ceaf-500b-9b18-43a009503aa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/5b81a39e884037b48cc84b49ea436d36dae717f1f0ddd10815028cf13cc845a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334241} {:answer/id #uuid "c41b3778-d91f-5566-b142-90c842b5e773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/c3f185adc76a4a7147c5206ac79f1c06ee9d5f96ee0f4364facc581ba243b3e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334242} {:answer/id #uuid "5aded0fa-0ac6-5431-a083-c8cd7e8bb883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/4c1f027a3361c9c3abd8b172ba92ef161c1ad7161d011f10cb6ff9653ff7cf4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334243} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a71ab3-6762-5bf8-b2bb-695e3304d7da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/ba2bcd2fc7d3680d1f81a3ed0470399114e3c76db6e0b80e4e845cc13859fc1a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8a71ab3-6762-5bf8-b2bb-695e3304d7da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/ba2bcd2fc7d3680d1f81a3ed0470399114e3c76db6e0b80e4e845cc13859fc1a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334244} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb832ac3-577f-55f3-92c6-31aac96cbada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3bf59515-ca53-5609-b8e1-36654671bf45" :question/math-academy-id "q-85672" :question/problem "For which of the following graphs would the trapezoidal rule give an underestimate of the area under the curve between $x=-2$ and $x=0?$" :question/worked-solution "The trapezoidal rule underestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave down over the given interval.     \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave down on $[-2,0]$ is the one shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/ba2bcd2fc7d3680d1f81a3ed0470399114e3c76db6e0b80e4e845cc13859fc1a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bec73486-156f-5124-97b4-58f65ea8430d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b7d057d-2da4-50fc-a2e2-c12adb52c2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334247} {:answer/id #uuid "18a9fed8-410d-5a29-82e3-e55afa16996b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334248} {:answer/id #uuid "86715dfd-5cf6-5ca2-b7ae-72fd16433f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334249} {:answer/id #uuid "0d91aabd-30b0-50fa-82e3-3f3359097d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334250} {:answer/id #uuid "5bcf7b9f-990f-5033-a1bd-682ddbe952d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{59}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b7d057d-2da4-50fc-a2e2-c12adb52c2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334247} :answer-field/id #uuid "92932307-2ea1-5bcb-9342-1e9d7e1a4216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85dbffc2-34a4-5adb-ba1c-5cb57f1c42d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-85725" :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-1,4]=[-1,0]\\cup [0,3]\\cup [3,4],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/48212ed7c5cf3d8b6e523a98b91634320b8684c889e55d62ce4043f082f83cde.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-1)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(3)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)+f(4)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{0+2}{2}\\cdot (0-(-1))+\\frac{2+4}{2}\\cdot (3-0)+\\frac{4+5}{2}\\cdot (4-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 3+\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+9+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bec73486-156f-5124-97b4-58f65ea8430d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60bc9e5a-5afe-57fd-9e36-910be76480fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334254} {:answer/id #uuid "b30fd017-161b-54ce-b34e-e7dc119d8fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334255} {:answer/id #uuid "b99d820a-8b32-5914-86b1-9f748434676e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334256} {:answer/id #uuid "5f656a96-f7da-5e61-a93a-cc21f634dd54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334257} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6bbe41-67be-5139-87d8-bfac4ecf8808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f656a96-f7da-5e61-a93a-cc21f634dd54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334257} :answer-field/id #uuid "c25f102d-e92c-51cd-b663-3c70508105be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79a1ba56-e0d3-53cc-9098-a11282eb05d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-85726" :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-3,3]=[-3,-2]\\cup [-2,0]\\cup [0,3],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $6$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055663/assets/3a9a6a7a340299c0b1f3fa349ad705c34079b74b0d3d1d1c3205d12ed9ef485d.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(-2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-2)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(3)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{6+4}{2}\\cdot (-2-(-3))+\\frac{4+3}{2}\\cdot (0-(-2))+\\frac{3+1}{2}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =5+7+6 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
